```javascript
// Define the model architecture
const model = tf.sequential();

model.add(tf.layers.dense({ units: 16, inputShape: [1], activation: 'relu' }));
model.add(tf.layers.dense({ units: 16, activation: 'relu' }));
model.add(tf.layers.dense({ units: 1, activation: 'linear' }));

// Compile the model
model.compile({
  optimizer: tf.train.adam(),
  loss: 'meanSquaredError'
});
```

3. Generate training data and train the model:

```javascript
// Generate training data
const xs = tf.tensor2d(Array.from({ length: 100 }, (_, i) => i + 1), [100, 1]);
const ys = tf.tensor2d(Array.from({ length: 100 }, Math.sqrt), [100, 1]);

// Train the model
async function trainModel() {
  await model.fit(xs, ys, {
    epochs: 500,
    callbacks: {
      onEpochEnd: (epoch, log) => console.log(`Epoch ${epoch}: loss = ${log.loss}`)
    }
  });
}

trainModel();
```

4. Use the trained model to predict square roots:

```javascript
async function predictSquareRoot(input) {
  const result = model.predict(tf.tensor2d([input], [1, 1]));
  return result.dataSync()[0];
}

// Example usage:
predictSquareRoot(9).then(sqrt => console.log(`The square root of 9 is approximately ${sqrt}`));
```

This code will create a simple neural network with two hidden layers, each containing 16 neurons, and a linear output layer. The model is trained using the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss function. After training, you can use the `predictSquareRoot` function to approximate square roots.
