Integrating an e-commerce site with Salesforce Commerce Cloud involves several steps, including setting up the Salesforce Commerce Cloud environment, configuring the e-commerce site to connect with Salesforce, and implementing the necessary code to handle data synchronization and user interactions. Below is a simplified example of how you might write TypeScript code to integrate an e-commerce site with Salesforce Commerce Cloud using the Salesforce Commerce Cloud REST API.

First, ensure you have the necessary Salesforce Commerce Cloud (formerly known as Commerce Cloud) and Salesforce CLI installed and configured on your system.

1. Install the Salesforce CLI and authenticate:

```bash
npm install -g sfdx
sfdx auth:web:login -d
```

2. Create a new Salesforce Commerce Cloud project:

```bash
sfdx create project commercecloud-project --manifest-name CommerceCloudProject.json
```

3. Install the Salesforce CLI plugin for TypeScript:

```bash
npm install -g @salesforce/sfdc-typescript-plugin
```

4. Initialize the project with TypeScript:

```bash
sfdx ts:init
```

5. Create a new TypeScript file for the integration (e.g., `commerce-integration.ts`):

```typescript
import { HttpClient } from 'salesforce-commercesdk';

// Replace with your Salesforce Commerce Cloud instance URL
const commerceCloudInstanceUrl = 'https://your-instance.salesforce.com';

// Replace with your Salesforce Commerce Cloud API key
const apiKey = 'your-api-key';

// Initialize the HTTP client with the Commerce Cloud instance URL and API key
const httpClient = new HttpClient({
  instanceUrl: commerceCloudInstanceUrl,
  apiKey: apiKey,
});

// Function to fetch products from Salesforce Commerce Cloud
async function fetchProducts(): Promise<any> {
  try {
    const response = await httpClient.get('/commerce/catalog/v1/products');
    return response.data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching products:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// Function to create a new product in Salesforce Commerce Cloud
async function createProduct(productData: any): Promise<any> {
  try {
    const response = await httpClient.post('/commerce/catalog/v1/products', productData);
    return response.data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error creating product:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// Function to update an existing product in Salesforce Commerce Cloud
async function updateProduct(productId: string, productData: any): Promise<any> {
  try {
    const response = await httpClient.put(`/commerce/catalog/v1/products/${productId}`, productData);
    return response.data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error updating product:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// Function to delete a product from Salesforce Commerce Cloud
async function deleteProduct(productId: string): Promise<void> {
  try {
    await httpClient.delete(`/commerce/catalog/v1/products/${productId}`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error deleting product:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

// Example usage
(async () => {
  try {
    const products = await fetchProducts();
    console.log('Fetched products:', products);

    const newProduct = {
      name: 'New Product',
      description: 'A new product description',
      price: 9.99,
      // Add other product details as needed
    };

    const createdProduct = await createProduct(newProduct);
    console.log('Created product:', createdProduct);

    const updatedProduct = {
      price: 7.99,
      // Update other product details as needed
    };

    await updateProduct(createdProduct.id, updatedProduct);
    console.log('Updated product:', updatedProduct);

    await deleteProduct(createdProduct.id);
    console.log('Deleted product:', createdProduct);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Integration error:', error);
  }
})();
```

This example demonstrates how to fetch products, create a new product, update an existing product, and delete a product from Salesforce Commerce Cloud using the Salesforce Commerce Cloud REST API. You'll need to replace the placeholders with your actual Salesforce Commerce Cloud instance URL, API key, and product details.

Remember to handle authentication and authorization as needed, depending on your specific use case and security requirements.
